  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 234 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nA fairly stagnant synoptic weather pattern will last through much of \nnext week. Looking at the big picture, ensembles are in strong \nagreement showing the upper level ridge migrating eastward, and \nsetting up over the southern Plains or southeastern US for much of \nnext week. A series of fairly potent upper level lows will move into \nthe West Coast throughout the period as well. That leaves our area \nunder steady southwest flow for much of the week. \n\nBeginning on Monday, model guidance depicts a notable shortwave \ntrough ejecting to the northeast out of the monsoon region. This \nwill be accompanied by a surge in moisture which should support \nanother round of scattered PM showers and thunderstorms. An early \nlook at the overall setup suggests a typical monsoonal thunderstorm \npattern with good forcing, but unimpressive instability. PoPs are \ncurrently around 30 to 50%, generally the scattered category, with \nlocally heavy rainfall possible due to precipitable water remaining \nquite high. The limited instability presents a failure mode though. \nSeveral ECMWF ensemble members show heavy rainfall failing to make \nit far enough north to reach our area, likely due to the lack of \ninstability, while GEFS members as a whole are more aggressive on \nthunderstorm coverage. Monday also looks like the coolest day of the \nweek, with highs generally near seasonal averages.\n\nBeyond Monday, precipitation chances look more limited but will not \ndisappear entirely. Precipitable water looks to remain elevated for \nmost of the week with the stubborn upper level pattern continuing to \nbring in a good fetch of monsoon moisture. Expect at least isolated \nPM showers and thunderstorms every day, but coverage should be more \nlimited than the more potent monsoon surge over the weekend. PoPs \nremain generally in the 10 to 20% range each day of the upcoming \nwork week. While the core of the upper level ridge will remain \nsoutheast of our area, expect a slow warming trend through the week, \nwith highs generally around 5 degrees above seasonal averages. \nSouthwest flow aloft will also support regular breezy to windy \nconditions in the typical areas of Carbon, Albany, and Converse \ncounties, leading to continued elevated fire weather conditions.",
